---
.title = "IRC on Rooms",
.description = "Read an IRC channel as a capability-secured room, or point an ordinary IRC client at a native Rooms directory: two edges, one CapTP machinery.",
.date = .date("2026-08-24T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

The [rooms app]($link.page('examples')) has two IRC edges. The bridge reads
an IRC channel as an ordinary room, so a Rooms client can follow it. The
front lets an ordinary IRC client enter rooms that live on a native
directory. Both are projections at the edge: inside, everything is the same
CapTP machinery every other Rooms client uses.

# Read an IRC channel as a room

```sh
zig build rooms-irc-bridge
```

The default follows Libera's `#ocapn` over TLS and serves the resulting
`libera/#ocapn` room on a loopback CapTP listener. The command prints the
listener port and the room name, and any Rooms client can join them. IRC
members appear as `irc/<nick>`, which keeps their origin visible on every
front.

The bridge is read-only until a registered nick opts into two-way mode with
its SASL password. Two-way requires TLS, paces outbound lines, and bounds
what it retains across a reconnect, so a busy room cannot flood the channel
or the process.

# Enter rooms with an IRC client

```sh
zig build rooms-irc-front -- --directory=127.0.0.1:9099
```

Point the IRC client at `127.0.0.1:6667`, pick a nickname, and
`JOIN #general`. Channel and direct `PRIVMSG`, `NAMES`, `LIST`, `PART`,
`PING`, `TOPIC`, and `WHOIS` all work. `JOIN` enters a room that exists on
the directory and reports the directory's refusal when there is none.

Each IRC connection holds the Directory, Room, and introduced direct-message
capabilities it acquired. A direct message from IRC therefore uses the same
introduction and retargeting rules as every other Rooms client, and the
endpoint another member redeems is Noise-sealed by default. The IRC client
itself cannot retain a capability, so the front remains the endpoint and
makes no end-to-end claim on its behalf. What IRC cannot represent, such as
files or overlong lines, becomes a notice rather than a partial message.

# What holds it up

The front admits a bounded number of clients, rooms, direct lines, and
queued writes, so one connection cannot grow the process. Automated tests open
a real socket and speak the same CapTP dialect Goblins expects, and they run
inside `zig build check`; `zig build goblins-rooms-irc` runs both edges live against a real
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/) vat, including a direct
message the vat answers. The
[IRC README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/apps/rooms/irc/README.md)
carries the full flag surface, deployment postures, and bounds.
